+library contracts.  A Phase80 adapter must consume one open, caller-owned
+`pietto.execution.ResultBatch`; it must never accept a `ResultHandle`, query,
+SQL, iterator or whole result.  `pyarrow.record_batch(batch)` is the existing
+standard C Data conversion and must be reused.  The adapter neither closes the
+batch nor opens a database; its returned object remains usable after the batch
+closes, without a zero-copy, RSS, device, mutability or thread-safety promise.
+
+The source guarantees select order, row order, multiplicity and a unique field
+label per selected column.  A closed batch already raises `PIE-R4002` through
+the public `ResultBatch` guard.  Current Float output is finite binary64;
+signed zero is retained, but NaN and infinity are refused before a batch is
+issued.  Current results do not admit generic Binary/dictionary/enum output.
+No target conversion may silently fill/drop/rename/reorder/flatten/stringify or
+JSON-encode a value.
+
+There is no existing public data-science adapter to reuse.  The relevant
+existing reuse is exactly `ResultBatch`, `pyarrow.record_batch(batch)`, and the
+private Arrow result/ownership owners as their producers—not as an alternate
+public input.  There is no reason for each adapter to add its own error layer:
+closed-batch failures retain their existing public error and missing optional
+packages or target conversion failures use the normal import/target exception
+until Gate1 specifies otherwise.
+
+`D75.03` still makes `compile_queries(...)` only a Phase80 Gate1 candidate.
+It is not needed to convert one batch and must not be introduced as an adapter
+convenience.  The recommended route defers it, along with database-native
+array/JSON, `postgres_adbc` nested output, Float `round(..., digits)`, and the
+analysis example.
+
+## Single recommended v1 contract
+
+Use one small lazy public module, with local imports at call time:
+
+```python
+from pietto.interop import (
+    plot_line,
+    plot_scatter,
+    to_numpy,
+    to_pandas,
+    to_polars,
+    to_scipy_csr,
+)
+
+def to_pandas(batch: ResultBatch) -> "pandas.DataFrame": ...
+def to_polars(batch: ResultBatch) -> "polars.DataFrame": ...
+def to_numpy(batch: ResultBatch, column: int) -> "numpy.ndarray": ...
+def to_scipy_csr(
+    batch: ResultBatch,
+    row: int,
+    column: int,
+    value: int,
+    shape: tuple[int, int],
+) -> "scipy.sparse.csr_matrix": ...
+def plot_line(
+    batch: ResultBatch, x: int, y: int, *, ax: "matplotlib.axes.Axes"
+) -> "matplotlib.lines.Line2D": ...
+def plot_scatter(
+    batch: ResultBatch, x: int, y: int, *, ax: "matplotlib.axes.Axes"
+) -> "matplotlib.collections.PathCollection": ...
+```
+
+This is one recommended complete initial type/loss contract, to approve as one
+document and then verify against the selected installed targets:
+
+| Consumer | Admit | Preserve / refuse |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| pandas, Polars | flat `Int`, `Bool`, finite `Float`, `Text`, including a typed empty batch | source field/row order, multiplicity and nulls; document the observed target dtype under its pin; refuse Decimal, Date, Timestamp, UUID and nested fields until individually admitted |
+| NumPy | one non-null `Int`, `Bool` or finite `Float` column | exact native dtype and shape, including typed empty and Float signed zero; refuse nullable, Text, Decimal, temporal, UUID and nested columns rather than introduce a mask/object convention |
+| SciPy | three non-null `Int64` columns for row, column and value | zero-based coordinates, explicit non-negative shape, no duplicate coordinates, stored zero distinct from missing; refuse all other types, negative/out-of-range indices and inferred shape |
+| Matplotlib | two non-null `Int` or finite `Float` columns and caller-supplied `Axes` | supplied order and returned artist identity; no sorting, aggregation, `show`, `save`, pyplot/current-Axes/backend selection or implicit figure ownership; refuse null, nonnumeric and nested inputs |
+
+Passing `ax` is required rather than using `ax=None`, so the API makes no
+hidden figure/backend ownership choice.  Positional selectors retain source
+order and avoid adding a second label-selection convention.  The narrow
+admission set is intentional: it makes all five libraries usable for the
+0.2.0 requirement while keeping every unproved conversion fail-closed.
+
+The current Arrow maintenance selection to consume is `pyarrow==26.0.1`.
+The checked-out result owner and package metadata still name `26.0.0`; this is
+not evidence against the maintenance selection and this report changes neither.
+The implementation should use the existing optional-dependency mechanism,
+keep imports lazy, and update the pinned package/install witnesses together;
+module subdivision, extra convenience groups and each target's mechanical pin
+are ordinary package implementation work, not further owner decisions.
+
+## The only two Gate1 choices
+
+1. **Approve the ResultBatch interoperability v1 type contract above.**  This
+   one approval covers the public signatures, pandas/Polars dtype/null witness
+   requirement, NumPy nullable refusal, SciPy coordinate/duplicate/zero law,
+   Matplotlib caller-Axes rule, all stated refusals, and no new adapter error
+   wrapper.  Each admitted row requires an installed-product witness; an
+   unproved target behaviour remains refused.
+2. **Confirm the recommended Phase80 scope boundary.**  Deliver only those
+   five one-batch adapters and defer `compile_queries(...)` and every other
+   listed Phase80 candidate.  Selecting any deferral is a new scope decision,
+   not a repair.
+
+## Reuse, verification, and route
+
+* Reuse `ResultBatch` and `docs/execution.md` for caller ownership and
+  post-close lifetime; reuse `project_arrow_result` as the sole checked schema
+  owner and `project_arrow_interop` only as its private C Data producer.
+* Extend the existing execution/result public and ownership tests for the
+  common boundary.  Add target tests by product function, not phase name.
+  Check core-only import, each selected optional installation, missing package,
+  closed batch, no handle/database open, order/multiplicity/null/refusal, typed
+  empty, signed zero, and post-close usability.  Package smoke and installed
+  consumers cover changed package inputs.  No database campaign follows from
+  one-batch conversion.
+* After Gate1, use six small slices: common public/lifetime/package boundary;
+  pandas+Polars; NumPy; SciPy; Matplotlib; package/docs/acquisition/audit.
+  This groups shared concerns without creating a framework.
+
+## Budget status
+
+No numeric `C/F/D/S` budget can be proposed truthfully from the current
+authority.  The repository's accessible records call `S` a scope-repair cap
+in particular phase decisions, and Phase76's `D` definition counts diagnostic
+commands; they do not define reusable Phase80 meanings for `C` or `F`, nor do
+they say that design/qualification commands count as `D`.  The budget draft is
+therefore `C=UNKNOWN, F=UNKNOWN, D=UNKNOWN, S=UNKNOWN`, with Gate1 required to
+define each unit and cap before a slice starts.  This report does not infer a
+count from historical phase budgets.
+
+No library was installed, no latest pin was queried, no test or database was
+run, and no repository file was changed.
